n='nsec-firewalls';nf=['https://wiki.nftables.org/wiki-nftables/index.php/Simple_ruleset_for_a_workstation'];nist=['https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-207.pdf'];aws=['https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/security-group-rules.html'];nat=['https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/nat-gateway-troubleshooting.html'];syn=['https://wiki.nftables.org/wiki-nftables/index.php/Synproxy']
edit(n,'The previous lesson showed that anyone can send almost any packet to almost anyone.','The previous lesson showed why receiving a packet does not, by itself, establish a trustworthy identity.','Avoidignorerouting/filteringconstraints.')
edit(n,'no network location should be trusted at all.','network location alone must not grant implicit trust.','Locationcaninformpolicywithoutimplicittrust.',nist)
edit(n,'Most firewalls evaluate rules **top to bottom, and the first match wins**.','The toy filter below uses **top-to-bottom, first-match evaluation**. Real products differ: AWS security groups combine allow rules, while nftables can continue across chains after an accept verdict.','Removeunsupportedmostandglobalfirstmatchmodel.',nf+aws)
edit(n,'Order bugs like this, and broad rules that shadow narrow ones, are the most common firewall mistakes.','Order bugs and broad rules that shadow narrow ones can contradict the intended policy.','Unsupportedmistakeranking.')
edit(n,'Inbound default-deny is universal. Outbound (egress) default-deny is rarer but valuable: malware that gets in usually needs to call home, and a server that can only reach its database and one update mirror is much harder to use for exfiltration.','Explicit inbound and outbound allowlists can reduce exposure. Egress restrictions can obstruct some command-and-control and exfiltration paths, but allowed services may still be abused; default-deny adoption is not universal.','Noevidenceuniversality/prevalenceandeffectnotguaranteed.')
edit(n,'To let replies out, a stateless filter needs a rule allowing a whole range of high ports, which is far broader than you meant.','A stateless filter needs a rule covering the intended return traffic. For general public clients this often includes a range of destination ephemeral ports; source ports, addresses and protocol flags may further constrain a rule, but do not prove connection state.','Statelesscanmatchsourceportandaddressnotonlywholehighportrange.')
edit(n,'Packets that belong to a known flow are matched against the table, not the rules:','Connection tracking supplies state that filtering rules can match. A known flow is accepted only when the applicable policy permits it:','Conntracknotautomaticrulebypass.',nf)
edit(n,'On Linux this is **conntrack**, and the typical nftables input chain starts by accepting known flows:','On Linux this is **conntrack**. This abbreviated nftables input-chain example explicitly accepts established and related traffic:','Toyconfigurationnotcompleteworkstationpolicy.',nf)
edit(n,'UDP and ICMP have no handshake, so a stateful firewall invents a pseudo-connection: "a request went out, so allow matching replies for a short time" (tens of seconds for UDP).','This is an input-chain fragment, not a complete deployment ruleset: it omits IPv6 neighbour discovery, router advertisements, other required ICMP handling, and forwarding/egress policy. Blindly applying it can break connectivity. UDP and ICMP tracking uses protocol-specific tuples and timeouts; a UDP reply does not establish application-level authentication.','OriginalinetexampledropsIPv6NDandcannotbecompletefirewall.',nf)
edit(n,'That is identity-based policy in miniature, and it survives instances changing IP address.','Within supported AWS connectivity arrangements, the reference resolves to associated interfaces\' private addresses and follows membership changes. It is a network policy reference, not cryptographic proof of application identity; traffic through a middlebox can require different rules.','SGreferenceslimitedaddress/pathscope.',aws)
edit(n,'Both need to see inside the traffic, which for TLS means either terminating it (a reverse proxy holding the server\'s key) or intercepting it (installing a corporate root certificate on clients).','Reading encrypted HTTP content requires authorised decryption, such as TLS termination or interception with a trust anchor accepted by the client. Some application identification instead uses exposed metadata; installing a root certificate alone does not intercept traffic.','Metadataidentificationdoesnotalwaysrequiredecryption.')
edit(n,'Attackers who get in rarely land where the data is; they **move laterally**.','An attacker may **move laterally** from the initial foothold toward other resources.','Removeunsupportedrarelyfrequency.')
edit(n,'Anything on the corporate network or VPN is trusted.','In this simplified perimeter-only model, internal location grants broad trust; real perimeter designs can also enforce internal controls.','Notalltraditionaldesignsflatimplicittrust.')
edit(n,'**Zero trust** (NIST SP 800-207) removes network location as a source of trust.','**Zero trust** (NIST SP 800-207) rejects trust based solely on network location.','Locationcanbecontextualsignal.',nist)
edit(n,'**Authenticate and authorise every request**, not every network connection, using the identity of the user and the service.','**Evaluate access to each resource** using subject and device identity and policy. NIST describes per-session access and ongoing reevaluation, not a requirement to make the user perform fresh MFA for every HTTP request.','CorrectNISTper-sessionversuseveryrequestrequirement.',nist)
edit(n,'**Least privilege**: grant access to one application, not to a network.','**Least privilege**: limit the resources and operations accessible for the task.','Resourcecanbenonapplicationnotexactlyone.',nist)
edit(n,'**Assume breach**: log everything, and keep sessions short so stolen credentials expire.','**Monitor and reevaluate**: collect relevant security telemetry and restrict or revoke access as risk changes. Shorter sessions limit some stolen-token exposure but do not automatically expire a stolen password or private key.','Sessionexpirydoesnotinvalidatecredential.',nist)
edit(n,"Google's **BeyondCorp**, built after the 2009 Aurora attacks, is the best-known implementation.","Google's **BeyondCorp** is an influential published implementation example, described in its 2014 paper.",'Removeunsupportedpopularityrankingandunverifiedhistoricalcausality.',['https://research.google.com/pubs/archive/43231.pdf'])
edit(n,'Between services, zero trust usually means **mutual TLS** with per-service identities, often handled by a service mesh (lesson 3).','Between services, **mutual TLS** can authenticate service identities, with authorisation enforced separately; a service mesh is one implementation option (lesson 3).','mTLSnotwholeZTnorprovedusualdeployment.',nist)
edit(n,'a host with a carefully filtered IPv4 address and an unfiltered global IPv6 address is reachable anyway.','a reachable IPv6 path can bypass IPv4-only filtering when a service listens on IPv6 and no other control blocks it.','Globaladdressalonenotrouting/listenerguarantee.')
edit(n,'Firewalls match rules, usually on the 5-tuple; first match wins and the default should be deny, ideally for outbound traffic too.','Firewall evaluation semantics vary. The toy model is first-match; choose explicit defaults and needed inbound/outbound allowances.','Alignfirstmatchscope.')
edit(n,'Stateful firewalls track connections so replies are allowed automatically; the state table is a resource attackers can exhaust.','Stateful firewalls track flows so policy can allow matching replies; the state table is a resource attackers can exhaust.','Trackingnotimplicitaccept.')
edit(n,'Zero trust moves trust from network location to verified identity and device on every request, and complements rather than replaces network controls.','Zero trust rejects trust based solely on location, evaluates resource access with identity and device context, and complements network controls.','AlignNISTscope.',nist)
qedit(n,2,'options.2.text','Which flows are in progress, allowing rules to match connection state','Statealoneisnotautomaticallow.',nf)
qedit(n,2,'options.2.explanation','Correct. State provides context for policy. An established/related accept rule can permit return traffic without a separate port-range rule, but tracking alone does not bypass filtering.','Alignstateversuspolicy.',nf)
