qedit(n,7,'options.2.text','A state-exhaustion flood; use protocol-appropriate admission controls, such as correctly configured SYN proxying for TCP, and review tracking limits/timeouts','HostSYNcookiesdonotprotectseparateconntrack.',syn)
qedit(n,7,'options.2.explanation','Correct. SYN proxying must avoid allocating normal tracked state for unverified SYNs. Host TCP cookies alone may leave a separate firewall table exposed; UDP floods need different controls.','Criticaldistinctionhostbacklogvsconntrack.',syn)
qedit(n,7,'workedExample','1. A full connection table can reject new flows despite spare link capacity.\n2. Identify which protocol and state entries are consuming it.\n3. For SYN floods, a correctly configured SYN proxy can validate clients before normal tracked-state allocation; nftables examples combine notrack and synproxy.\n4. Tune limits/timeouts within memory and service requirements; a larger table alone is not prevention.\n5. Selective tracking bypass may suit some traffic only with an explicit stateless policy and no required stateful NAT/dependent rules. It is not a blanket DNS fix.','Rejectunsafeblanketbypassandnoentryclaims.',syn)
